<h2>The prosecution case</h2>

<p>The applicant arrived at Terminal 3 of IGI Airport from Tanzania on 8 April 2022 and was intercepted by Customs. He stated that he had ingested capsules containing narcotics and was taken to hospital, where 59 capsules were recovered between 8 and 10 April 2022 across four panchnamas. The substance tested positive for cocaine with a net weight of about 702 grams — well above the commercial-quantity threshold. He was formally arrested on 14 April 2022, and charges were framed under Sections 21(c) and 23(c) of the NDPS Act, the case being registered through Customs at Police Station Customs, IGI Airport. The bail application was moved under Section 483 of the BNSS.</p>

<h2>How Section 37 was answered</h2>

<p>The judgment, reserved on 14 October and pronounced on 16 October 2026, works through the two limbs of Section 37 rather than around them.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Integrity of the recovery</h3><p>The court noted procedural discrepancies in the seizure and chain of custody, spread across the four panchnamas, together with delay in sampling — matters going to the core of a case built on recovery.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Antecedents</h3><p>The applicant had no criminal antecedents, bearing on the second limb — the likelihood of offending while on bail.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Custody against trial progress</h3><p>About four years and six months in custody, with only 13 of 33 prosecution witnesses examined, meant the trial had no early end in sight.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The court held the applicant "has made out the requirement of Section 37 of the NDPS Act" and granted bail on conditions.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The conditions imposed</h2>

<p>Bail in commercial-quantity cases, when granted, is tightly fenced. The court required a personal bond of Rs 1,00,000 with two sureties in the like amount verified by the Special Court, with a lien on the sureties' property; appearance on every date; a functional mobile number and verified addresses, with any change notified on affidavit; surrender of the passport and no departure from India without the Special Court's permission; no inducement, threat or tampering, and no offence while on bail; and an affidavit disclosing source of income and bank account details. Because the applicant is a foreign national, the State must notify the Foreign Registration Officer, the applicant must register with the FRRO within a week and inform the investigating officer, and the High Commission of Tanzania is to be informed.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weight in the judgment</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Commercial quantity (≈702 g cocaine)</td><td>Triggered Section 37 — bail required satisfying the twin conditions</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Discrepancies in seizure, chain of custody, sampling delay</td><td>Supplied prima facie "reasonable grounds" on the not-guilty limb</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No antecedents</td><td>Supported the unlikely-to-offend limb</td></tr>
  <tr><td>4 years 6 months custody; 13 of 33 witnesses done</td><td>Prolonged incarceration reinforced release</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Foreign national</td><td>Managed through passport surrender, FRRO registration and notification conditions, not by refusing bail</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>

<p>Two features deserve attention. First, the judgment treats foreign nationality as a risk to be managed by conditions rather than a disqualification — an approach consistent with the principle that bail decisions rest on individual assessment, not categories. Second, it illustrates the now well-settled interaction between Section 37 and the constitutional guarantee of a speedy trial: the statutory rigour holds where the prosecution case is facially sound and the trial moves, but recovery-stage discrepancies combined with years of custody and a distant trial horizon can satisfy even the twin conditions. Accused persons and their families should note that the route to such orders runs through the record — panchnama comparison, sampling timelines, FSL dates and witness progress — all of which must be placed before the court with precision.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This article summarises a Delhi High Court judgment for general information. Findings at the bail stage are prima facie and do not determine guilt or the outcome of the trial. Nothing here is legal advice.</p></div>
